{"records":[{"id":"8xhv88c7wt","title":"Genome-wide screening in human kidney organoids identifies developmental and disease-related aspects of nephrogenesis","description":"Human organoids allow the study of proliferation, lineage specification, and three-dimensional tissue development. Here, we present a genome-wide CRISPR screen in iPSC-derived kidney organoids. The combination of inducible genome editing, longitudinal sampling and endpoint sorting of tubular and stromal cells generated a complex, high-quality dataset uncovering a broad spectrum of insightful biology from early development to ‘adult’ epithelial morphogenesis. Our functional dataset allows improving mesoderm induction by ROCK inhibition, contains monogenetic and complex trait kidney disease genes, confirms two additional CAKUT genes (CCDC170 and MYH7B), and provides a large candidate list of ciliopathy-related genes. Finally, the identification of a cis-inhibitory effect of Jagged1 controlling epithelial proliferation shows how mosaic knockouts in pooled CRISPR screening can discover ways of communication between heterogeneous cell populations in complex tissues. Collectively, these data serve both as a rich resource for the kidney community and as a benchmark for future iPSC-derived organoid CRISPR screens.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-29T13:48:59.335Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8xhv88c7wt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8xhv88c7wt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rosemarie","last_name":"Ungricht","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Philipp","last_name":"Hoppe","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Screening","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"sxrvzkr4hh","title":"Pooled in vitro and in vivo CRISPR-Cas9 screening identifies tumor suppressors in human colon organoids","description":"UMI validation screen, original data from in vivo tumor suppressor CRISPR/Cas9 screening experiment. Figure 6.\nExperimental setup: 281 tumor suppressor gRNAs, positive controls, neutral controls and gRNAs targeting essential genes were introduced into the CRISPR-UMI lentivirus (Michlits et al., 2017). Transduced AK organoids (APC-KO and KRASG12D) were transplanted subcutaneously in NSG mice followed by barcode sequencing of the tumor pool (from 10 mice) and 5 individual tumors. Clone and read number were determined by next generation sequencing. The conventional gRNA enrichment analysis was compared to UMI-CRISPR-screening with outlier removal.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-04-20T15:46:17.868Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/sxrvzkr4hh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/sxrvzkr4hh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Henner","last_name":"Farin","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xvk2gk4hw3","title":"Data of Murine Trophoblast Organoids as a Model for Trophoblast Development and CRISPR/Cas9 Screening","description":"Raw data for figures in Murine Trophoblast Organoids as a Model for Trophoblast Development and CRISPR/Cas9 Screening","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-25T15:27:04.321Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xvk2gk4hw3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xvk2gk4hw3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Qian","last_name":"Mao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Developmental Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y8dndzn3mg","title":"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication","description":"These data are part of the Supplemental Items associated with the manuscript entitled \"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication\". These files include Supplementary Data file 1-4.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-16T10:34:13.579Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y8dndzn3mg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y8dndzn3mg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Limeng","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Screening","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7ch42j6767","title":"Long term expandable mouse and human induced nephron progenitor cells enable organoid modeling of kidney maturation, plasticity and disease","description":"Nephron progenitor cells (NPCs) self-renew and differentiate into nephrons, the functional unit of the kidney. Here, manipulation of p38 and YAP activity allowed for long-term clonal expansion of primary mouse and human NPCs, and induced NPCs (iNPCs) from human pluripotent stem cells. Molecular analyses demonstrated cultured iNPCs resemble closely primary human NPCs. iNPCs generated nephron organoids with minimal off-target cell types and enhanced maturation of podocytes relative to published human kidney organoid protocols. Surprisingly, NPC culture medium uncovered plasticity in human podocyte programs, enabling podocyte reprogramming to an NPC-like state. Scalability and ease of genome-editing facilitated genome-wide CRISPR screening in NPC culture, uncovering novel genes associated with kidney development and dis-ease. Further, NPC-directed modeling of autosomal-dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD) identified a small molecule inhibitor of cystogenesis. These findings highlight a broad application for the reported iNPC platform in the study of kidney development, disease, plasticity, and regeneration. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-03-15T14:23:53.448Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7ch42j6767","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7ch42j6767"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"BIAO","last_name":"HUANG","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zipeng","last_name":"Zeng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sunghyun ","last_name":"Kim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Connor-James ","last_name":"Fausto","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kari ","last_name":"Koppitch","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kenneth","last_name":"Hallows","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nils","last_name":"Lindstrom","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Andrew","last_name":"McMahon","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhongwei","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Gel Electrophoresis","Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f2zxx5rprm","title":"RNA seq data for Measles virus infection in human intestinal organoids","description":"RNA seq results data for Measles virus infection in human intestinal organoids, including mock, infection and treated groups","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-08-27T14:29:19.731Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f2zxx5rprm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f2zxx5rprm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jiangrong","last_name":"Zhou","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["RNA Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"835fcfbvhs","title":"Preparation of CRISPR/Cas9 and gRNA plasmids","description":"Human genome engineering has been transformed by the introduction of the CRISPR (clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats)/Cas (CRISPR-associated) system found in most bacteria and archaea. Type II CRISPR/Cas systems have been engineered to induce RNA-guided genome editing in human cells, where small RNAs function together with Cas9 nucleases for sequence-specific cleavage of target sequences. Here we describe the protocol for Cas9-mediated human genome engineering, including construct building and transfection methods necessary for delivering Cas9 and guide RNA (gRNA) into human-induced pluripotent stem cells (hiPSCs) and HEK293 cells. Following genome editing, we also describe methods to assess genome editing effi- ciency using next-generation sequencing and isolate monoclonal hiPSCs with the desired modifications for downstream applications. Keywords: genome engineering • CRISPR • human stem cells","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2016-12-12T12:58:15.307Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/835fcfbvhs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/835fcfbvhs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"George","last_name":"Church","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Experimental Method"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vtvxykv2cr","title":"2020_Cell reports resubmission tables","description":"All tables associated with our paper titled \"High throughput CRISPR screening identifies genes involved in macrophage viability and inflammatory pathways\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-12-15T08:44:50.23Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vtvxykv2cr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vtvxykv2cr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Susan","last_name":"Carpenter","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Macrophage","Immunity","Innate Immunity","Screening","Pooled Analysis","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"x7hxm36xbp","title":"Molecular Heterogeneity of Breast Milk","description":"the data of the article titled Molecular Heterogeneity of Breast Milk in Intestinal Development: A Multi-Omics Approach Using Human Organoids and Caco-2 Cells.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-05-06T18:36:05.441Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/x7hxm36xbp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/x7hxm36xbp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yao","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Analysis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gbdfwg325y","title":"Bacteriostatic antibiotics promote CRISPR-Cas adaptive immunity against phages by enabling increased spacer acquisition","description":"Phages impose strong selection on bacteria to evolve resistance against viral predation. Bacteria can rapidly evolve phage resistance via receptor mutation, or using their CRISPR-Cas adaptive immune systems. Acquisition of CRISPR immunity relies on the insertion of a phage-derived sequence into CRISPR arrays in the bacterial genome. Using Pseudomonas aeruginosa and its phage DMS3vir as a model, we demonstrate that conditions that reduce bacterial growth rates, such as exposure to bacteriostatic antibiotics (which inhibit cell growth without killing), promote the evolution of CRISPR immunity. We demonstrate that this is due to slower phage development under those conditions, which provides more time for cells to acquire phage-derived sequences and mount an immune response. Our data reveal that the speed of phage development is a key determinant of the evolution of CRISPR immunity, and suggest that use of bacteriostatic antibiotics can trigger elevated levels of CRISPR immunity in human-associated and natural environments. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-04T13:32:56.091Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gbdfwg325y","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gbdfwg325y"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Tatiana","last_name":"Dimitriu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microbiology","Antibiotics","Bacteriophage","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":135195,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":137,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":135195,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":134501,"restricted":68,"embargoed":626},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2270},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1333},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":654},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":474},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":439},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":416},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":403},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":381},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":323},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":269}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":132564},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1900},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":185},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":136},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":77},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":64},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":56},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":40},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":31},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia M